<<<
concernment has no senses that are not as well, & now more naturally & frequently, expressed by the noun concern; the substitution of the latter was censured as affectation in the 17th c., but the boot is now on the other leg, & c. should be dropped as a NEEDLESS VARIANT.
===
**concernment** has no senses that are not as well, & now more naturally & frequently, expressed by the noun *concern*; the substitution of the latter was censured as affectation in the 17th c., but the boot is now on the other leg, & *c.* should be dropped as a ^^NEEDLESS VARIANT^^.
>>>

